{"type":"message","message":{"id":"msg_23df5744-16c0-4798-8441-613f43cb2517","role":"user","created":1775351174,"content":[{"type":"toolResponse","id":"call_e192369b8c3a439293ee1206","toolResult":{"status":"success","value":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1960_01.txt:The average rate of discount for the 166-day bills was 4.726 percent. Tenders of $2,016 million were accepted, including noncompetitive tenders for $430,000 or less from any one bidder of $368 million, which were accepted in full at the average price of accepted competitive bids.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1960_01.txt:On the 366-day bills the average rate of discount was 5.067 percent. Tenders of $1,502 million were accepted. For this issue also, noncompetitive tenders of $400,000 or less from any one bidder were accepted in full at the average price of accepted competitive bids. The amount of noncompetitive tenders was $350 million.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1960_01.txt:Regular weekly issues of Treasury bills totaled $8.2 billion in December, refunding the total and weekly amounts of the maturing 13-week and 26-week bills. The five issues of 91-day bills amounted to $5.8 billion and the five 182-day issues to $2.4 billion. Of the new 13-week issues, those for December 3 and 31 were for $1.1 billion each and each of the others, for $1.2 billion. Four of the 26-week issues were for $0.5 billion and the issue for December 3 was for $0.4 billion. Average rates of discount on the new bills were as follows:\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1960_01.txt:| 13-week (Additional amount of bills of original maturity of 26 weeks) > Date of --- > Additional issue | 13-week (Additional amount of bills of original maturity of 26 weeks) > Date of --- > Original issue | 26-week > Approximate equivalent annual average rate > Unnamed: 2_level_2 | 26-week > Date of issue > Unnamed: 3_level_2 | Unnamed: 4_level_0 > Approximate equivalent annual average rate > Unnamed: 4_lev